<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kwantung Army</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kwantung Army was a group of the Imperial Japanese Army stationed in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from the early twentieth century. Its officers staged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukden-incident">Mukden Incident</a> of 1931, which led to the occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, and often acted independently of the government in Tokyo. Its insubordination illustrates the military's growing control over policy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> before its defeat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kwantung-army.jpg" alt="Kwantung Army" width="320" height="124" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kwantung_Army_Headquarters.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…atomic bombing of Hiroshima on 6 August 1945 and Nagasaki on 9 August, combined with the Soviet declaration of war on 8 August and the rapid destruction of the <b>Kwantung Army</b> in Manchuria, finally provided Emperor Hirohito with the external shock necessary to override institutional resistance to surrender.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Russo-Japanese War Japan took Russia's place: the Treaty of Portsmouth gave the <b>Kwantung Army</b> the right to occupy southern Manchuria, and the South Manchurian Railway, founded in 1906, dominated the economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/manchukuo">Manchukuo · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The garrison, renamed the <b>Kwantung Army</b> in 1919, acted with growing independence and in 1931 staged the Mukden Incident, leading to the creation of Manchukuo.</blockquote><a href="/state/kwantung-leased-territory">Kwantung Leased Territory</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince Demchugdongrub (De Wang) led an Inner Mongolian movement for autonomy from China and turned to the <b>Kwantung Army</b> for support.</blockquote><a href="/state/mengjiang">Mengjiang</a></li></ul></section>
